Save93 equipment comparison at 42 FPS with the target raised to 84: catch-up off against accelerate with the skip rule,
2026-11-11 13:19-13:29
Test DLL APBGameplayTrace.dll SHA-256 6DF3C7534F0A5ECCDD66F4E4E493F24879ED0720B9805E5F1FA2F1E7CB6F9A76 (12:26 build).
Command: build/catchup/run-effect-ab.ps1 -Prefix sk42t84 -Limits 42 -Target 84 -TraceMode catchup-ab -SkipBlocked -Repeat 2
In the release plugin this is [VMCatchUp] Mode=accelerate, SkipWaitingCalls=true, TargetFPS=84. At 42 FPS a target of 84 asks
for one extra VM cycle in every frame; the default target of 60 asks for three in seven frames.
Two launches of eight trials, window in front 100% in all 16 trials, guard_violations 0, no "turned off" line.
Run folders build/catchup/sk42t84-42-1, sk42t84-42-2. Set beside the target-60 run of the same build and scene, made ten
minutes earlier (verification/Save93-VMCatchUp-SkipBlocked-42fps.log); the two targets were not compared within one launch.

RESULT (target 84; in brackets the target-60 run)
- Queue wait of ALL queued native calls, pooled over both launches:
    off: 19,618 calls, mean wait 23.1 ms, same frame 9.4%, two frames or more 2.8%   [24.2 ms, 9.2%, 3.1%]
    on:  21,308 calls, mean wait 14.7 ms, same frame 36.3%, two frames or more 1.0%  [17.7 ms, 24.1%, 1.5%]
  36% less waiting per queued call [27%]. Per trial the mean is 21.6-24.9 ms off and 14.3-15.4 ms on.
  The same-frame share equals what the rule reached at 30 FPS with target 60 (36%), where it also ran in every frame.
- Equipment drain time, trials with 174 handlers: off 253-325 ms (6 trials, median 277), on 180-206 ms (8 trials, median 202),
  27% shorter. The on trials are now below the ~250 ms at which both conditions sat with target 60. The two other off trials
  had 176 and 202 handlers (252 and 633 ms) and are left out.
- Extra cycles ran in 333-334 of 334-335 frames in six of the eight on trials. In the last trial of each launch they ran in
  215 and 229 frames: there the queue front could not run in 77 and 90 frames and the queue was empty after 8 and 15 cycles.
- Cost: 0.076-0.115 ms per frame, at most 0.66 ms in one frame [0.046-0.058, 0.70]. Engine frame time p99 25.8 ms off and
  on in both launches; the longest frame was 26.7 / 26.5 ms off and 26.7 / 43.2 ms on (one frame of 1,339 in the second launch;
  what took the time is not known).
- tests/analyze_gameplay_trace.py stopped on the second launch with "PresentMon rows do not form pairs", so that folder's
  summary has no PresentMon section. The plugin's own data (vm.log, vm-events.csv, engine-render.csv) is complete and is what
  the figures above use.

READING
- At 42 FPS, raising TargetFPS to twice the frame rate brings the option's effect to where it was at 30 FPS: the limit at
  target 60 was the number of extra cycles, not the rule. The cost per frame doubles with the number of cycles and stays near
  0.1 ms.
- It makes the VM run 84 times a second at 42 FPS, more often than the game itself would at 60 FPS (though no more than it
  does at a real 84 FPS). Nothing went wrong in 16 trials of one scene; quest progression was only tested at 30 FPS with
  target 60.

